=== 1. HOW MANY ROWS PER RESPONDENT DOES EACH REGRESSION USE? ===

The review's headline defect (A) is that the repeated measures are not
modelled. That is true of the ANOVA. It is worth establishing, rather than
assuming, whether it is also true of the nine regressions.

    dataset rows respondents max_rows_per_respondent
     M1_rep  149         149                       1
     M1_pop  189         189                       1
   M1_brand  153         153                       1
     M2_rep  196         196                       1
     M2_pop  143         143                       1
   M2_brand  152         152                       1
     M3_rep  491         491                       1
     M3_pop  491         491                       1
   M3_brand  491         491                       1
 M ANOVA_RM 1473         491                       3

Every regression takes **one** measurement per respondent: each person
contributes a single y for a given cue, so inside any one of the nine
regressions the observations are independent and ordinary least squares is
the right estimator with the right standard errors. The clustering problem
exists in exactly one place -- the ANOVA, the only analysis that stacks all
three measurements -- and that is the one where Error(lfdn) failed.


=== 2. DO M1 AND M3 ESTIMATE THE SAME QUANTITY? ===

M1 restricts to n = 1. M3 keeps every observation but carries comp_i and
x_i * comp_i, so its x_i coefficient is the effect at comp_i = 0 -- which is
n = 1 again. The two are routes to the same estimand.

   cue M1_estimate  M1_se M3_estimate  M3_se pooled_estimate pooled_se
 brand      1.5289 0.2355      1.5028 0.2409          1.4392    0.2199
   rep      0.4986 0.2626      0.4576 0.2690          0.6837    0.2216
   pop      0.3611 0.2148      0.3677 0.2184          0.4502    0.1967

M1 and M3 agree to within a few hundredths, and their standard errors are
almost identical: M3 buys no precision on this quantity, because the
first-exposure effect is still identified by the same first-position
observations. What M3 adds is the moderation by comparison, which is a
second question rather than a better answer to the first.

Mean |M1 - M3| across the three cues: 0.0246
Mean standard error: M1 0.2376, M3 0.2428, one pooled model 0.2128

The precision gain comes from pooling across CUES, not across positions:
one model shares the residual variance, the respondent variance and the
involvement coefficients across all 1,473 answers.


=== 3. WHAT ONLY A POOLED MODEL CAN DO ===

Nine regressions produce nine coefficient vectors with no covariance
between them. Any statement of the form 'cue A beats cue B' needs that
covariance. This is the whole of the gap, and it is not a matter of taste:
it is what makes the thesis's central claim untestable as written.

 focal_pairwise position estimate t.ratio  p.value
    brand - rep    first   0.7555  2.4188 0.031422
    brand - pop    first   0.9890  3.3488 0.002505
      rep - pop    first   0.2335  0.7879 0.430874

Same quantity M1 targets, plus the comparison M1 cannot make.


=== 4. WHAT M2'S INTERACTIONS ACTUALLY ASK ===

x_j and x_k are not two more attributes of the app being judged. They are
attributes of the OTHER apps, seen on earlier slides. So 'interaction
between focal variables' here means: does the effect of this app's rating
depend on how strong the brand of a previous app was? That is a carryover
moderation, not a factorial interaction -- and a factorial interaction is
what the abstract's wording suggests to a reader.

Respondents per combination of the two non-focal levels, for i = rep:
       x_pop
x_brand   0   1
      0 112 123
      1 132 124

Those cells carry the six interaction terms. With 143-196 observations and
11 predictors the minimum detectable interaction is 0.76-0.98 s.d.
(05_power_m2_interactions.csv): the question was asked on a sample that
could not answer it.

Observations M2 leaves unused: 491 of the 982 measurements that have
at least one app before them -- every n = 2 answer. The same carryover
question on all 982 is 10_sequence_effects.R.


=== 5. WHAT comp_i COLLAPSES ===

M3 codes comparison as first (0) versus second-or-third (1). The effects by
position say the two halves of that second level are not alike:

 focal position estimate    SE  p.value
 brand    first    1.439 0.220 8.68e-11
   rep    first    0.684 0.222 2.08e-03
   pop    first    0.450 0.197 2.23e-02
 brand   second    0.881 0.198 9.64e-06
   rep   second    0.635 0.224 4.63e-03
   pop   second   -0.217 0.214 3.12e-01
 brand    third    0.918 0.219 3.06e-05
   rep    third    1.128 0.194 7.53e-09
   pop    third    0.397 0.226 7.96e-02

Reputation is 0.63 on the second app and 1.13 on the third; popularity is
-0.22 and +0.40. Averaging them into one 'after comparison' number is not
wrong, but it describes a situation no respondent was ever in.


=== 6. VERDICT ON THE THREE SPECIFICATIONS ===

M1  Sound. Right estimand (uncontaminated first judgement), right
    estimator, valid standard errors, and the model the thesis says it
    relies on. Its limit is 149-189 observations by construction, which
    makes the involvement moderations underpowered.

M3  The most efficient use of the data among the three, and the one closest
    to what this review recommends: all 491 measurements of a cue, with
    comparison as a moderator. Two caveats: its x_i coefficient is the
    effect at comp_i = 0 and was read as an overall effect, and pooling
    positions 2 and 3 into one 'after comparison' level hides that the two
    differ for reputation.

M2  The weakest of the three. The question is legitimate and interesting,
    but it is asked on a third of the data, it collapses the order of the
    two earlier apps, and its power is an order of magnitude short of the
    effects it looks for. The null it produced carries no information.

ANOVA  The only analysis that needed the repeated-measures machinery, and
    the only one that got it wrong. Its stated role is preliminary -- 'may
    help in understanding if some effects exist' -- but its three-way
    interaction was then used as 'further foundations for subsequent
    analyses', which is more weight than a screening device can carry.

What none of the three can do, individually or together, is compare one cue
with another. That is the finding of this review, and it is a statement
about the SET of models, not about any one of them.
